// binary search, closest pair`,label:"Splittable, combinable"},{code:"// fetch one URL",label:"Single request"},{code:"// increment a counter",label:"Single operation"},{code:"// render a fixed banner",label:"No input at all"}],correct:0,explanation:"The pattern needs two things: a split that makes genuinely smaller subproblems of the SAME kind, and a combine step cheaper than solving from scratch. Sorting and searching have both. A single request has no meaningful split, and the last two have no subproblems at all — dividing them creates work rather than saving it.",hint:"What must be true about the pieces for splitting to be worth anything?"}],31:[{type:"trace",prompt:"How many times is fib() entered for fib(5), no memo?",code:[{text:"function fib(n) {"},{text:"    if (n <= 1) return n;"},{text:"    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);"},{text:"}"},{text:"// count every call in the tree for fib(5)",highlight:!0}],options:["5","9","15","25"],correct:2,explanation:"Count bottom-up: calls(0)=1, calls(1)=1, then calls(n)=1+calls(n-1)+calls(n-2) → 3, 5, 9, 15. Fifteen calls to compute six values — fib(1) alone is entered five times. That redundancy is the whole case for memoisation, which collapses the tree to one call per distinct n.",hint:"Build a small table: how many calls does fib(2) take? fib(3)? Each row uses the two before it."},{type:"refactor",prompt:"Which rewrite stops recomputing the same values?",originalCode:`function fib(n) {
