// Apply to apps, APIs, open source`,label:"Build projects! 🚀"},{code:"// Review everything",label:"Review"},{code:"// Just read more",label:"More books"},{code:"// Stop forever",label:"Never stop!"}],correct:0,explanation:"Courses teach recognition; projects force RECALL under ambiguity — no multiple choice, no highlighted line. That gap is where skill forms. Confidence follows shipping; it does not precede it."},{type:"pair",prompt:"Your journey recap",pairs:[{id:1,left:"CH1-2",right:"Variables & Flow"},{id:2,left:"CH3-4",right:"Loops & Functions"},{id:3,left:"CH5-7",right:"Data Structures"},{id:4,left:"CH8-10",right:"Algorithms & DP"}],explanation:"The order was the argument: structures before algorithms because algorithms are choices ABOUT structures. Anything foggy backward from here points at which chapter to revisit."},{type:"trace",prompt:"Final puzzle: what does this return?",code:[{text:"function mystery(n) {"},{text:"    if (n === 0) return 1;"},{text:"    return n * mystery(n - 1);"},{text:"}"},{text:"console.log(mystery(5));",highlight:!0}],options:["5","15","120","720"],correct:2,explanation:"5·4·3·2·1 = 120 — factorial, unnamed. Reading a recurrence and recognising the function it defines is the exact skill this course was building. 15 is the SUM 1..5; 720 is 6!, one boundary off.",hint:"Base case 1, each level multiplies by n. Which well-known function is that?"},{type:"scramble",prompt:"Final: build memoized fibonacci",pieces:[{id:"a",text:"function fib(n, memo = {}) {"},{id:"b",text:"    if (n in memo) return memo[n];"},{id:"c",text:"    if (n <= 1) return n;"},{id:"d",text:"    memo[n] = fib(n-1, memo) + fib(n-2, memo);"},{id:"e",text:"    return memo[n];"},{id:"f",text:"}"}],distractors:[{id:"g",text:"    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);"}],correctOrder:["a","b","c","d","e","f"],explanation:"The memo-as-default-parameter idiom works in JS because default expressions are re-evaluated per top-level call yet the SAME object threads through the recursion. Check memo first, store before returning — the two moves that turn 2ⁿ into n.",hint:"Which line makes the second call to fib(3) free?"},{type:"output",prompt:"What does this log?",code:[{text:"const a = [3, 1, 2];"},{text:"const b = [...a].sort((x, y) => x - y);"},{text:"const c = a.sort((x, y) => x - y);"},{text:"console.log(b, c === a);"}],terminalOutput:"[ 1, 2, 3 ] true",options:["[1, 2, 3] true","[1, 2, 3] false","[3, 1, 2] true","[1, 2, 3] undefined"],correct:0,explanation:"sort returns the SAME array it sorted, not a copy — so c === a is true and a is now sorted too. Only the spread made an independent copy. This is the difference from Python, where sort() returns None and sorted() returns a new list; JavaScript returning the array makes chaining pleasant and mutation easy to miss.",hint:"Does sort hand back a new array or the one it was called on?"},{type:"pair",prompt:"Match each complexity to a familiar operation",pairs:[{id:1,left:"O(1)",right:"Map lookup"},{id:2,left:"O(log n)",right:"Binary search"},{id:3,left:"O(n log n)",right:"Sorting"},{id:4,left:"O(n²)",right:"Comparing every pair"}],explanation:"These four cover most of what you meet. The gaps between them are enormous at scale: on a million items that is roughly 1, 20, 20 million and a trillion operations. Knowing which class a piece of code falls into is usually more useful than knowing its exact constant.",hint:null},{type:"realworld",prompt:"You have finished the journey. What is the fastest way to keep the skill?",scenario:"🎓 Ten chapters done. What now?",options:[{code:`// read code you did not write,
// and predict before you run`,label:"Read unfamiliar code"},{code:"// reread these chapters",label:"Reread the chapters"},{code:"// memorise Big-O tables",label:"Memorise complexities"},{code:"// only write new code",label:"Only write, never read"}],correct:0,explanation:"This whole app trains one thing: predicting what code does before running it. That skill transfers only if you keep aiming it at code you have not seen — an open-source pull request, a colleague's diff, your own code from six months ago. Rereading what you already know builds recognition rather than reading skill, tables without context fade, and writing alone never exposes you to unfamiliar styles.",hint:"What was every exercise here actually asking you to do?"}]},ik={1:[{type:"trace",prompt:"What is the value of `count`?",code:[{text:"int count = 0;"},{text:"for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {"},{text:"    if (i % 2 == 0) {"},{text:"        count++;"},{text:"    }"},{text:"}"},{text:"System.out.println(count);",highlight:!0}],options:["2","3","5","4"],correct:1,explanation:"Five values of i (0..4), three even (0, 2, 4) — the usual slip is forgetting 0 counts as even. Note count++ is the idiomatic Java increment; count += 1 is identical in effect.",hint:"Write down what i is on each pass, then tick the even ones."},{type:"realworld",prompt:"Which code counts total items in a cart?",scenario:"🛒 Cart: 3 apples, 2 oranges, 1 banana.",options:[{code:`int[] cart = {3, 2, 1};
