// table: iteration, computes everything up to n`,label:"Direction and stack usage"},{code:"// the memo version is asymptotically faster",label:"The memo is faster"},{code:"// the table version gives different answers",label:"They differ in results"},{code:"// the memo uses less memory, always",label:"Memo saves memory"}],correct:0,explanation:"Both are O(n) time and produce identical answers — the differences are operational. Top-down computes only subproblems actually reached, but deep chains risk StackOverflowError. Bottom-up never touches the stack, uses a flat primitive array instead of a boxing HashMap, and often admits an O(1)-space squeeze. In Java the iteration + int[] combination is usually the pragmatic winner.",hint:"Rule out the claims about speed and answers first. What operational differences remain?"},{type:"trace",prompt:"Extend the table: what is dp[4]?",code:[{text:"// dp[1]=1, dp[2]=2"},{text:"// dp[3]=dp[2]+dp[1]=3"},{text:"// dp[4]=dp[3]+dp[2]=?",highlight:!0}],options:["4","5","6","8"],correct:1,explanation:"3 + 2 = 5. It is Fibonacci wearing a costume — same recurrence, shifted start. Most counting-paths problems reduce to a known recurrence like this; recognising the shape saves deriving it from scratch. 8 is dp[5], one row further: the classic off-by-one.",hint:"The last two rows are on screen. Add them."},{type:"output",prompt:"How many times is a NEW value computed for n = 30?",code:[{text:"static Map<Integer,Long> memo = new HashMap<>();"},{text:"static long fib(int n) {"},{text:"    if (n < 2) return n;"},{text:"    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);"},{text:"    long r = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);"},{text:"    memo.put(n, r);"},{text:"    return r;"},{text:"}"}],terminalOutput:"29",options:["29","2692537","59","900"],correct:0,explanation:"Only 2 through 30 ever reach the computing line — 29 values — and everything else is served from the map, against 2,692,537 calls without it. Careful with what you count: the method is still ENTERED 59 times, because a cache hit enters, checks and returns early. The algorithm is unchanged; only how often it runs is.",hint:"With a cache, how many DIFFERENT values of n can fib actually be asked about?"},{type:"bug",prompt:"This cache never hits. Which line is missing its counterpart?",code:[{text:"static Map<Integer,Long> memo = new HashMap<>();",hasError:!1},{text:"static long fib(int n) {",hasError:!1},{text:"    if (n < 2) return n;",hasError:!1},{text:"    long r = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);",hasError:!1},{text:"    memo.put(n, r);",hasError:!0},{text:"    return r;",hasError:!1}],correctLine:4,explanation:"The result is stored but never READ — there is no containsKey check before the work, so every call recomputes and the map just grows. A cache is two halves: check before, store after. Writing only the store half is the most common memoisation mistake, and it looks completely reasonable in review.",hint:"The map is written on line 5. Where is it ever read?"},{type:"complexity",prompt:"Fibonacci: plain recursion against memoised",optionA:{code:`static long fib(int n) {
