// from {1, 5, 10, 25}`,label:"Coin change"},{code:"// reverse a list",label:"Reverse a list"},{code:"// merge sort a list",label:"Merge sort"},{code:"// find the largest element",label:"Find the maximum"}],correct:0,explanation:"Coin change asks the same question over and over — the best way to make 38p comes up on many different paths — so caching it collapses the work. That is overlapping subproblems plus optimal substructure, the two conditions DP needs. Merge sort is the instructive wrong answer: it HAS substructure but its halves never overlap, so a cache would never hit. Reversing and finding a maximum are single linear passes with no subproblems to reuse.",hint:"Merge sort splits into subproblems too. Which of these asks the SAME smaller question more than once?"}],32:[{type:"trace",prompt:"What does power(2, 10) return?",code:[{text:"long power(long b, int e) {"},{text:"    if (e==0) return 1;"},{text:"    if (e%2==0) { long h=power(b,e/2); return h*h; }"},{text:"    return b*power(b,e-1);"},{text:"}"},{text:"// power(2, 10) = ?",highlight:!0}],options:["20","100","512","1024"],correct:3,explanation:"2¹⁰ = 1024 — the algorithm changes the route, not the value. 20 is 2×10, 100 is 10², 512 is 2⁹, one halving misplaced. The long return type is no accident: int silently overflows at 2³¹, and fast power reaches big numbers fast, so the wide type matters.",hint:"The recursion computes 2 to the 10th, just cleverly. What IS 2¹⁰?"},{type:"scramble",prompt:"Assemble the coin-change DP",pieces:[{id:"a",text:"int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {"},{id:"b",text:"    int[] dp = new int[amount+1];"},{id:"c",text:"    Arrays.fill(dp, amount+1);"},{id:"d",text:"    dp[0] = 0;"},{id:"e",text:"    for (int i=1; i<=amount; i++)"},{id:"f",text:"        for (int c : coins)"},{id:"g",text:"            if (c<=i) dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i-c]+1);"},{id:"h",text:"    return dp[amount]>amount ? -1 : dp[amount];"},{id:"i",text:"}"}],distractors:[{id:"j",text:"    dp[0] = 1;"}],correctOrder:["a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h","i"],explanation:'amount+1 plays the role of infinity — no real answer can use more than `amount` coins, so anything above it means "unreachable", which the last line converts to -1. Integer.MAX_VALUE would overflow to negative on the +1. The fill must precede dp[0]=0, or the zero gets stamped over. The spare line seeds dp[0]=1: every answer one too high, no crash to warn you.',hint:"Why amount+1 rather than Integer.MAX_VALUE as the sentinel? What happens to MAX_VALUE + 1?"},{type:"realworld",prompt:"Fib benefits from DP; merge sort does not. What is the difference?",scenario:"🧠 Both recurse. Only one is worth caching.",options:[{code:`// fib(n-1) and fib(n-2) RESHARE subcalls
// merge sort halves never overlap`,label:"Overlapping subproblems"},{code:"// fib is arithmetic, sorting is not",label:"Arithmetic vs rearranging"},{code:"// fib recurses twice, merge sort once",label:"Number of recursive calls"},{code:"// fib is defined on integers",label:"Integer inputs"}],correct:0,explanation:"Caching pays only when the same subproblem RECURS: fib(5) and fib(4) both need fib(3). Merge sort's halves are disjoint — no subarray is sorted twice, so a cache would only ever miss. That plus optimal substructure is the two-part test for DP. Merge sort also recurses twice, which kills the third option.",hint:"A cache helps only if something is asked twice. Which algorithm ever repeats a subproblem?"},{type:"output",prompt:"What does this print?",code:[{text:"static List<String> perms(String s) {"},{text:"    if (s.length() <= 1) return List.of(s);"},{text:"    List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();"},{text:"    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++)"},{text:"        for (String p : perms(s.substring(0,i) + s.substring(i+1)))"},{text:"            out.add(s.charAt(i) + p);"},{text:"    return out;"},{text:"}"},{text:'System.out.println(perms("abcd").size());'}],terminalOutput:"24",options:["24","4","16","12"],correct:0,explanation:"Every position can host every remaining character, so the count is n factorial — 4! = 24. Factorial growth is brutal: 10 characters is 3.6 million and 15 is over a trillion, which is why permutation search is only viable on tiny inputs.",hint:"Four choices for the first character, then three for the next. Keep multiplying."},{type:"bug",prompt:"This flatten overflows the stack. Which line is the real problem?",code:[{text:"static List<Object> flatten(List<Object> x) {",hasError:!1},{text:"    List<Object> out = new ArrayList<>();",hasError:!1},{text:"    for (Object i : x) {",hasError:!1},{text:"        if (i instanceof List) out.addAll(flatten(x));",hasError:!0},{text:"        else out.add(i);",hasError:!1},{text:"    }",hasError:!1}],correctLine:3,explanation:"The recursive call passes x — the whole list — instead of i, the nested element. So it never gets smaller and recurses until StackOverflowError. The subproblem must be strictly smaller than the problem; passing the same argument back is the fastest way to guarantee it is not.",hint:"What is being passed to the recursive call, and is it smaller than what came in?"},{type:"complexity",prompt:"Checking every subset of n items",optionA:{code:`for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
